=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported "DNS down" complaint is a false premise — the campus DNS infrastructure is fully operational. The BIND9 daemon (`named`, PID 30) is running on srv1 (10.5.0.4), listening on port 53 on its external interface, and answering authoritative queries for the `jeremysitlab.com` zone correctly. The zone file (`/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab`) is syntactically valid and contains a wildcard `*` A record that maps every hostname under `jeremysitlab.com` to 10.5.0.4, so all internal campus names resolve as expected.

Every client tested — pc3 (Office B, 10.4.0.x), pc2 (Office A, 10.1.0.x), pc1, and laptop1 — has `nameserver 10.5.0.4` correctly set in `/etc/resolv.conf`, can reach srv1 with zero packet loss via ICMP, and successfully resolves both `srv1.jeremysitlab.com` and arbitrary hostnames such as `intranet.jeremysitlab.com` using the system resolver. The "recursion not available" notice in nslookup output is expected and intentional: `named.conf` sets `recursion no` because the server is authoritative-only for the local zone — it does not prevent resolution of `jeremysitlab.com` names and is not a fault.

The users appear to have misdiagnosed the symptom. Because they "have not tested anything by IP," the underlying issue they are experiencing — if any — is likely at the application or service layer on the hosts they are trying to reach, not in DNS. No corrective action is needed on the DNS server or any network device; the helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false alarm and the users redirected to test connectivity by IP to identify the real cause of their service failures.
=== END ===